## Why This Is Asked
Examines ability to tame CPU scheduling and memory reclaim in multi-tenant Linux deployments under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 cpu.max and CPU weight for per-tenant QoS
- cpuset and CPU affinity to confine tenants
- memory reclaim behavior and swappiness tuning
- BPF-based observability for per-tenant latency, IRQs, cache misses
- phased testing, safe rollback, and controlled rollouts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight latency probe template (pseudo)
async function measureLatency(endpoint){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await fetch(endpoint);
  return performance.now() - t0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify safe rollback criteria during staged tests?
- Which metrics dashboards and alerts would you configure for sustained SLOs?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-time network paths require precise tail latency insight without burdening performance. eBPF offers low-overhead, per-event visibility ideal for diagnosing bursts in UDP data paths.

## Key Concepts

- eBPF tracing (bpftrace/bpfcc) for live latency data
- Tracepoints vs kprobes for kernel events
- Per-CPU histograms and BPF maps for aggregation
- Data-path isolation with tc and minimal instrumentation
- Safe rollback/guardrails if overhead grows

## Code Example

```javascript
function percentile(arr, p){
  if (!arr.length) return 0;
  const sorted = [...arr].sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const idx = Math.ceil((sorted.length-1)*p/100);
  return sorted[idx];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this plan if TLS/DTLS terminates before the RX path?
- What thresholds and sampling rates would you choose for a fleet of hosts, and how would you automate rollback across them?
